    "https://www.jobs.ac.uk/job/DTC103/student-discipline-adviser": {
      "title": "Student Discipline Adviser",
      "description": "Student Discipline Adviser at University of Warwick This site uses cookies to store information on your computer. Some of these cookies are essential, while others help us to improve your experience by providing insights into how the site is being used. For more detailed information about the cookies we use, please see our Cookie policy I Accept Cookies I Do Not Accept Cookies Necessary Cookies Necessary cookies enable core functionality such as page navigation and access to secure areas. The website cannot function properly without these cookies, and can only be disabled by changing your browser preferences. Functional Cookies These help us to personalise the website to you by remembering your preferences and settings. functional On Off Marketing Cookies Marketing cookies help us provide personalised and relevant services or advertising to our website visitors, and to assess the effectiveness of our marketing campaigns. marketing On Off Analytics Cookies Analytical cookies help us to improve our website by collecting and reporting information on its usage. analytics On Off About this tool (Opens in a new window) Skip to main content Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice Jobs by Email Advertise a Job Recruiters Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice Jobs by Email Advertise a Job Your Account Recruiters Back to search results Student Discipline Adviser University of Warwick - Conduct and Resolution - Student Discipline Location: Coventry, University of Warwick Salary: £47,389 to £56,535\n                                                                                        per annum. Hours: Full Time Contract Type: Permanent Placed On: 29th September 2026 Closes: 12th October 2026 Job Ref: 345 Closing Date: Monday, 12 October 2026, 11:59PM (UK). About the Role This role is our lead in-house investigator for student cases involving allegations of sexual misconduct at the University of Warwick. The role of Assistant Registrar – Conduct and Resolution works within the Conduct and Resolution Team and acts as an Investigating Officer for a variety of student misconduct cases arising from complaints made by a wide range of external and internal stakeholders. Working alongside other investigators, both internal and external to the University, this post will take the lead on complaints and investigations relating to matters of sexual misconduct. Working closely with a wide range of stakeholders from across the institution, the postholder will act as a key point of contact on a range of Student Conduct issues, managing cases and investigations within agreed timescales and in accordance with the University’s Regulations and processes as well as the requirements of the Office for Students and external best practice from bodies such as Universities UK and the Office of the Independent Adjudicator. For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description (link here) . About You To succeed in this role, the postholder will need to have experience of conducting investigations into complex and sensitive matters, including sexual misconduct, with a proven ability to gather and weigh evidence and interact with reporting and responding parties, witnesses and their supporters in a trauma informed and professional manner. The postholder will manage multiple competing priorities, including multiple concurrent cases that cover a wide range of issues and that are often complex in nature. A flexible approach is required in order to meet the changing needs of the service, as well as a proactive approach to problem solving with due consideration of potential risks and adverse consequences. Good written and verbal communication skills and attention to detail are essential, as the postholder will be required to explain/present complex information in an accessible and user-friendly way. The postholder need to be able to demonstrate that they can conduct interviews with reporting parties, respondents and witnesses with tact and diplomacy. Due to the nature of the role the postholder will be required to demonstrate that they can manage highly sensitive and confidential information with tact and discretion. Full details of the duties and selection criteria for this role can be found in the vacancy advert on the University of Warwick's jobs pages. You will be routed to this when you click on the 'Apply' button. Share on social media We value your feedback on the quality of our adverts.\n            If you have a comment to make about the overall quality of this advert,\n            or its categorisation then please send us your feedback Advert information Type / Role: Subject Area(s): Location(s): Job tools Save this job Login to Save Job Max Saved Jobs Reached × Max Saved Jobs Reached A maximum of 500 Saved Jobs can be created against your account. Please remove an existing Saved Job in order to add a new Saved Job. Manage Saved Jobs × Save Job Please sign in or register for an account to save a job. Sign In Register More jobs from University of Warwick Early Years Practitioner (Nursery Assistant) Mechanical Maintenance Manager Assistant Campus Cleaning Services Manager Associate Professor (Human-AI Teaming) Research Fellow Assistant Clinical Professor Show all jobs for this employer … More jobs like this Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Consultant School Education and Quality Manager Assessment Manager College EDI Manager Research Officer Join in and follow us Copyright © jobs.ac.uk 1998 - 2026 Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice Jobs by Email Advertise a Job About Us Contact Us Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice Jobs by Email Advertise a Job About Us Contact Us Terms of use Privacy Policy Cookie Policy Accessibility Statement Terms of use Privacy Policy Cookie Policy Accessibility Statement Copyright © jobs.ac.uk 1998 - 2026 IE11 Trigger Browser Upgrade Recommended jobs.ac.uk has been optimised for the latest browsers. For the best user experience, we recommend viewing jobs.ac.uk on one of the following:"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.ac.uk/job/DTE751/marie-curie-early-stage-researcher": {
      "title": "Marie Curie Early Stage Researcher",
      "description": "Marie Curie Early Stage Researcher at University of Warwick Skip to main content Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice PhDs by Email Advertise a Job Recruiters Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice PhDs by Email Advertise a Job Your Account Recruiters Back to search results Marie Curie Early Stage Researcher University of Warwick - School of Engineering Qualification Type: PhD Location: Coventry, University of Warwick Funding for: UK Students, EU Students, International Students Funding amount: £39,600\n                                                                                        per annum (specific salary as per Marie Curie rate) Hours: Full Time Placed On: 7th October 2026 Closes: 1st November 2026 Reference: 383 About the Role Please note: the salary advertised covers a range of allowances and pension enrolments. Final salary will be dependent on entitlement to these allowances. The School of Engineering is looking to appoint a Early Stage Marie Curie Researcher to undertake high-quality research within the MSCA Doctoral Network POSEIDON ( https://poseidon-dn.eu/ ). The postholder will be assisting in developing the next generation of ecofriendly offshore windfarms and closely collaborating with academic and industrial partners across the EU to deliver the scientific and training objectives of the project. The successful candidate will undertake innovative research focused on addressing key challenges in sustainable offshore wind development, contributing to improved understanding, design and performance of offshore wind infrastructure and its interaction with the marine environment. They will also have opportunities to develop their research skills through collaboration across the POSEIDON network, international secondments and dedicated training activities. This post will have heavy emphasis on data science & ML programming applied to large environmental datasets (geospatial, weather, tide). This is an excellent opportunity for an early-career researcher to gain international research experience, develop a strong academic and industrial network, and contribute to the transition towards more sustainable offshore renewable energy. For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description ( link here ). About You A MSc or equivalent in Civil/Geotechnical Engineering, Ocean/Offshore Engineering, Applied Mechanics, Naval Architecture, or a closely related discipline You should evidence the ability to deliver independent research, and strong potential for high-quality research, with a developing profile and an understanding of relevant methods and techniques. Strong numerical and computational skills, plus proficiency in at least one of: Python, MATLAB, or C++, particularly for data analysis, automation, or machine learning implementation is essential, as is previous experience with computational mechanics and laboratory and physical tests for soil characterisation. You will be a collaborative and motivated individual, able to work effectively with partners across Europe and willing to undertake international secondments. Strong communication skills (in English), excellent IT proficiency, and the ability to deliver work on time are essential. Candidates will be required to meet Marie Curie Early Stage Researcher eligibility criteria which includes: At, applicants must not have been awarded a PhD. Candidates should not have lived or carried out their main activity in the UK for more than 12 months in the 3 years immediately before their recruitment. Applicants must be in the first four years (full-time equivalent research experience) of their research career You will be registered for a PhD degree at the University of Warwick. Full details of the duties and selection criteria for this role can be found in the vacancy advert on the University of Warwick's jobs pages. You will be routed to this when you click on the 'Apply' button. Share on social media We value your feedback on the quality of our adverts.\n            If you have a comment to make about the overall quality of this advert,\n            or its categorisation then please send us your feedback Advert information Type / Role: Subject Area(s): Location(s): PhD tools PhD Alert Created Job Alert Created Your PhD alert has been successfully created for this search. Your job alert has been successfully created for this search. Ok Ok PhD Alert Created Job Alert Created Your PhD alert has been successfully created for this search. Your job alert has been successfully created for this search. Manage your job alerts Manage your job alerts Account Verification Missing In order to create multiple job alerts, you must first verify your email address to complete your account creation Request verification email Request verification email jobs.ac.uk Account Required In order to create multiple alerts, you must create a jobs.ac.uk jobseeker account Create Account Create Account Alert Creation Failed Unfortunately, your account is currently blocked. Please login to unblock your account. Login Login Email Address Blocked We received a delivery failure message when attempting to send you an email and therefore your email address has been blocked. You will not receive job alerts until your email address is unblocked. To do so, please choose from one of the two options below. Re-verify your email Update your email Re-verify your email Update your email Max Alerts Reached A maximum of 5 Job Alerts can be created against your account. Please remove an existing alert in order to create this new Job Alert Manage your job alerts Manage your job alerts Creation Failed Unfortunately, your alert was not created at this time. Please try again. Ok Ok Create PhD Alert Create Job Alert When you create this PhD alert we will email you a selection of PhDs matching your criteria. When you create this job alert we will email you a selection of jobs matching your criteria. Our Terms and Conditions and Privacy Po